CONC = [
("h1", "Conclusion"),
("p", "The volume began with a plain statement: it argues a position. The position is that Malachi promises one Elijah the prophet before the day of the LORD, that the Gospels answer in three voices, and that the text points to a further coming. Gabriel says that John will go before the Lord in the spirit and power of Elias. John, asked by priests and Levites whether he is Elias, says, “I am not.” The Lord says, “if ye will receive it, this is Elias, which was for to come,” and after John’s death says, “Elias truly shall first come, and restore all things,” and then, “Elias is come already, and they knew him not.” The three voices are three statements made in three settings, and the volume has held each in its own place."),
("p", "It has read the offer of Matthew 11:14 as a conditional one made to Israel in John’s day. It has read the refusal in the verses that follow, where this generation is likened to children who would neither dance nor mourn, and in the end of John in the prison. It has read the restoring of all things as work not reported done, and the day of the LORD as not yet come. The text therefore points to a further coming of Elijah before the day, in Israel’s line, as the restorer. The volume has set Revelation 11 beside the position and listed what the chapter itself says, and it has not named the witnesses. It has not read the Body of Christ into any of the passages."),
("ctbl", "The position in one table.", [
    ("Question", "The position", "Chief passages"),
    ("Who is promised?", "Elijah the prophet, sent before the great and dreadful day of the LORD", "Mal 4:5, 6"),
    ("Who is the messenger of Malachi 3:1?", "John, as the Lord applies the text; the paragraph after it is not reported met", "Mal 3:1–5; Matt 11:10; Luke 7:27"),
    ("What did Gabriel say?", "John goes before the Lord in the spirit and power of Elias, to turn hearts and make ready a people", "Luke 1:16, 17; 2 Kings 2:15"),
    ("What did John say?", "“I am not” to the priests and Levites; he is the voice of Isaiah 40:3", "John 1:19–28; 3:28–30"),
    ("What did the Lord say?", "“If ye will receive it, this is Elias”; Elias shall first come and restore all things; Elias is come already", "Matt 11:14; 17:11, 12; Mark 9:12, 13"),
    ("Was the restoring done?", "It is not reported done; restitution is spoken of as ahead", "Acts 3:21"),
    ("What do the people’s lists show?", "John and Elias stand as two persons after John’s death", "Matt 14:1, 2; 16:13, 14; 27:47–49"),
    ("What follows?", "A further coming of Elijah before the day, in Israel’s line; the witnesses are not named", "Mal 4:5; Rev 11:3–12"),
  ], [0.26, 0.48, 0.26]),
("p", "The volume asks to be tested. A reader who disagrees may take any row of the table and look for the verse that overturns it. If the verse is there, the position must change. If it is not, the position stands on what the text says."),
("p", "It has tried to be fair to the readings it opposes. It has granted that Luke 1:17 speaks of an office and a spirit, that John was not the returned Tishbite in the sense the priests asked about, and that the Lord’s “Elias is come already” is a real statement and not to be set aside. It has named three writers who hold that a work of Elijah remains, and said where it parts from each, and two sources that hold the promise closed in John, and said where it agrees and where it does not. What it hopes the reader takes away is a habit: when the Lord says two things about one name, to read both in the order he said them, and to ask of each what it says, to whom, and when."),
]

